                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "According to the website/Linkedin of Kyvos Studio, they're a company of three people, so I'd cut them some slack on making such an ambitious game, but my gamedev class required I write an honest review so I won't take that into account.\n\nThis is not a good game. The mechanics are well-used but still serviceable. I've played many games in middle school where you change your color to land on platforms or where you run constantly and avoid obstacles, so it had a chance at being worth the price of downloading it and putting it in your library.\n\nThe problems are the controls and the level design. First, the controls are very slow to respond which is a problem in a reflex-based game. The majority of my deaths (of which there were over 100 in the first level) were not from inexperience or learning curve, but because the ball wouldn't change color in time or I would jump as soon as I touched a platform because the jumping is programmed like a My First Unity Project. Speaking of fractions of a second, the turrets in the game seem designed to shoot at just the right time to kill you,. (It's actually just a lack of testing which would have immediately shown how rage-inducing it could be.) There were platforms too far away to drop down to but too near to jump to, unintuitive boost pads (go under them), and countless little points where you died not from challenge, but because very little effort was put into beta-testing.\n\nAnd that's the real problem. They advertise this as a hard game for real gamers, but what they don't say is all the difficulty is in over-coming the bad game design. Yes, technically trying to play Red Light Green Light with Hellen Keller is hard, but not in a way that's fun.",
